## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a CGO boundary around a stateful C RNG and reason about thread safety when exposed to Go goroutines. Emphasizes memory safety, cross-language data types, and proper synchronization.

## Key Concepts
- CGO binding and C types
- Thread safety and synchronization across Go boundaries
- Managing C static state and seeding
- Performance considerations of locking in hot loops

## Code Example
```c
// rng.h
#pragma once
#include <stdint.h>
void seed_rng(uint64_t seed);
uint64_t next_rand(void);
```

```c
// rng.c
#include "rng.h"
#include <stdint.h>
static uint64_t s0 = 88172645463325252ull, s1 = 0x6a09e667f3bcc909ull;
void seed_rng(uint64_t seed) { s0 = seed; s1 = ~seed; }
uint64_t next_rand(void) {
  uint64_t x = s0; uint64_t y = s1;
  s0 = y;
  x ^= x << 23; x ^= x >> 17; x ^= y ^ (y >> 26);
  s1 = x;
  return s0 + s1;
}
```

```go
// rng.go
package rng
/*
#include "rng.h"
*/
import "C"
import "sync"

var _mu sync.Mutex

func NextRandom() (uint64, error) {
  _mu.Lock()
  defer _mu.Unlock()
  return uint64(C.next_rand()), nil
}
```

```go
// main.go
package main
import (
  "fmt"
  "sync"
  "example/rng" // adjust to actual module path
)

func main() {
  var wg sync.WaitGroup
  ch := make(chan uint64, 1000)
  for i := 0; i < 4; i++ {
    wg.Add(1)
    go func() {
      defer wg.Done()
      for j := 0; j < 1000; j++ {
        v, _ := rng.NextRandom()
        ch <- v
      }
    }()
  }
  go func() { wg.Wait(); close(ch) }()
  var min, max uint64 = ^uint64(0), 0
  for v := range ch {
    if v < min { min = v }
    if v > max { max = v }
  }
  fmt.Printf("min=%d max=%d\n", min, max)
}
```

